WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.893
- [Narrator] Over $150 billion.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.760
In three days, that's how much the world's

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.420
15 largest cryptocurrencies
lost in market value.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.450
It's because of the Crypto
Exchange platform FTX,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.183
which is behind this token, named FTT.

00:00:15.183 --> 00:00:18.900
On November 6th, the
token's value began to fall,

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.193
losing more than 80% of its
worth in the span of 72 hours.

00:00:23.193 --> 00:00:26.014
Once seen as a survivor
in a struggling market,

00:00:26.014 --> 00:00:28.710
the fall of FTX has sent shockwaves

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.020
through the cryptocurrency industry.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.853
So, what went wrong?

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.430
FTX is the brainchild
of Sam Bankman-Fried.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.267
He's commonly known as
SBF on social media.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.380
He's been previously hailed as a savior

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.270
of the crypto industry.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.103
- You were called the JP Morgan of crypto.

00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:47.936
- [Sam] Yep.

00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:49.680
- Does that bother you or not?

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.084
- It doesn't bother me too much.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.230
- [Narrator] Bankman-Fried founded

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.537
the quantitative trading firm
Alameda Research in 2017.

00:00:55.537 --> 00:00:59.048
Two years later, he started
FTX, an exchange platform

00:00:59.048 --> 00:01:01.800
for buying or selling cryptocurrencies.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.070
Right now, he's the majority
owner of both firms.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.844
- That kinda aroused some
skepticism among industry players

00:01:08.844 --> 00:01:11.100
and traders, investors, stakeholders,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.856
that there could be some
sort of conflict of interest

00:01:13.856 --> 00:01:17.040
in terms of whether research was getting

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.010
preferential treatment on FTX, vice versa.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.550
But the official narrative
that SBF gave in the past

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.150
is that the two companies
are separate entities.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.230
- [Narrator] After its launch,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.530
FTX attracted major
investments from Silicon Valley

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.820
and Wall Street.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.380
- Yeah, we'd raised a few billion dollars

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.870
over the course of the last couple years

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.490
and we're a profitable business.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.550
- [Narrator] It grew
into the fourth largest

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.630
cryptocurrency exchange
for derivatives trading.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.766
Celebrities promoted the platform in ads.

00:01:44.766 --> 00:01:46.813
- I'm getting into crypto with FTX.

00:01:46.813 --> 00:01:47.646
You in?

00:01:47.646 --> 00:01:48.600
- It's FTX.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.879
It's a safe and easy
way to get into crypto.

00:01:50.879 --> 00:01:52.140
- I don't think so.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.060
- [Narrator] FTX was gaining steam,

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.940
and in the process, often
tussled with Binance,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.504
the world's largest
crypto exchange by volume.

00:01:59.504 --> 00:02:04.230
- When FTX was getting started,
Binance invested in FTX

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.310
and it was one of the crypto
exchange's earliest investors.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.977
But FTX, as we know, grew really rapidly

00:02:11.977 --> 00:02:16.977
and became a very
substantial rival to Binance.

00:02:17.566 --> 00:02:19.582
- [Narrator] As FTX grew in the industry,

00:02:19.582 --> 00:02:23.058
Bankman-Fried furthered his
reputation as a Crypto savior,

00:02:23.058 --> 00:02:26.880
when digital asset prices
collapsed earlier this year.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.262
- It's not fair to customers.

00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:30.000
- [Narrator] He bailed out firms,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.009
spending about a billion dollars.

00:02:32.009 --> 00:02:35.447
- It's okay to do a deal
that is moderately bad

00:02:35.447 --> 00:02:37.230
in bailing out a place.

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.022
- [Narrator] But that image didn't last.

00:02:39.022 --> 00:02:42.150
On November 2nd, CoinDesk
published a report

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.670
based on a leaked Alameda balance sheet.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.340
According to the leaked
data, Alameda claimed it had

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.015
over 14 billion in assets
at the end of June,

00:02:51.015 --> 00:02:54.210
but most of that was FTX's tokens.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.351
Alameda CEO Caroline Ellison tweeted

00:02:56.351 --> 00:02:59.100
that the balance sheet wasn't complete.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.250
- Caroline also said
its financial situation

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.310
is under control, the
company is doing well.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.224
However, it seems like the market

00:03:07.224 --> 00:03:09.540
just didn't really buy that,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.220
and then traders continue
to withdraw from FTX.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.320
- [Narrator] FTX and
Alameda did not respond

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.060
to a request for comment.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.280
Things escalated on November 6th,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.020
when Binance said it would offload

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.259
hundreds of millions of dollars of FTT.

00:03:24.259 --> 00:03:27.450
Binance did not respond
to a request for comment.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:29.880
The announcement sparked mass withdrawals.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.050
That day, FTX processed $4
billion of transactions,

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.054
many times the normal amount for a day.

00:03:36.054 --> 00:03:39.870
Some got backlogged, which
sparked demand for more.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.010
By November 7th, that number
ballooned to $6 billion.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:47.947
On the 8th, a day later,
FTX's finances were in crisis.

00:03:47.947 --> 00:03:51.360
Binance stepped in and said
it would buy the company.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.050
It seemed like FTX might have
solved its liquidity problem.

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.120
But on the 9th, Binance backed out

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.799
of the non-binding acquisition.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:01.710
The next day, The
Journal reported that FTX

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.470
used money from customers
to fund risky bets

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.571
made by Alameda.

00:04:05.571 --> 00:04:08.305
- It's a shocking revelation
for a lot of people

00:04:08.305 --> 00:04:09.750
in the industry,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.560
because even though there
has been a lot of speculation

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:17.929
about FTX and Alameda research
being joined at the hip,

00:04:17.929 --> 00:04:21.711
nobody could have foreseen
that SBF was willing

00:04:21.711 --> 00:04:26.135
to transfer billions of customer funds

00:04:26.135 --> 00:04:31.050
at his crypto exchange to
help his crypto trading firm.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.820
- [Narrator] The Securities
and Exchange Commission

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.670
and Justice Department
are investigating FTX,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.040
according to a person
familiar with the matter.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.310
- This is not like the New
York Stock Exchange or Nasdaq.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.190
They take people's money,
they borrow against it.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.702
It's not much disclosure.

00:04:46.702 --> 00:04:49.980
And then they trade
against their customers.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.930
- [Narrator] A spokesperson
for the Justice Department

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.430
declined to comment.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.710
Bankman-Fried told investors that FTX

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:58.140
couldn't cover withdrawals
since its collateral

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.762
was dropping in value and
couldn't be liquidated,

00:05:00.762 --> 00:05:03.330
according to people
familiar with the matter.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.998
On the 11th, Bankman-Fried resigned as CEO

00:05:05.998 --> 00:05:09.380
and FTX and Alameda filed for bankruptcy.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:13.350
Afterwards, FTX said it was
probing a potential hack.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.361
More than 370 million
worth of crypto funds

00:05:16.361 --> 00:05:18.060
appeared to be missing,

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.580
according to crypto
analytics firm Elliptic.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.280
On the 12th, The Journal
reported that Alameda

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.464
and FTX executives knew that FTX

00:05:25.464 --> 00:05:28.620
had lent its customers money to Alameda.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.730
- So a lot of retail traders
and investors I've talked to

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:36.120
are feeling desperate or frustrated.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.632
The majority of the people in the industry

00:05:38.632 --> 00:05:40.350
could not have seen this coming

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:43.252
because FTX is such a dominant player.

00:05:43.252 --> 00:05:45.450
- [Narrator] The fallout
has led other companies

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.007
to tout their reserves and
call for more transparency

00:05:48.007 --> 00:05:49.860
in the industry.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.130
As of November 11th, according
to the bankruptcy filing,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.890
FTX is estimation of their liabilities

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.500
would make it the largest
crypto related bankruptcy

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.673
ever filed.

